v(236, ('In the time study, why were the keys for earlier and later reversed?', 'To compare arrangements that fit a person’s representation of time with ones that do not.', 'To keep earlier on the same side for the whole experiment for every person.', 'To avoid measuring reaction time, which differs between the two languages.', 'To make the task equally unfamiliar for English and Mandarin speakers.'),
       ('What is a canonicality effect?', 'Faster responding when the key arrangement fits how a person represents time.', 'Faster responding when the photographs are familiar to the person.', 'Slower responding when the keys are arranged from left to right.', 'Slower responding when a person answers in a second language.'))
v(237, ('In the time study, accuracy was high and speed was not gained by giving up accuracy. What does this strengthen?', 'The speed differences between arrangements are real effects of how time is represented.', 'Every cultural influence was assigned at random.', 'All groups had the same response times.', 'No spatial association was measured.'),
       ('Which arrangement was faster for both English and Mandarin speakers?', 'Earlier on the left.', 'Earlier on the right.', 'Earlier on the bottom.', 'No arrangement was faster.'))
